{"chunk_id": "5822b4893090", "text": "On a stormy November night, Victor Frankenstein finally succeeds in animating the creature he has assembled after nearly two years of obsessive labor. The moment of triumph instantly becomes horror: the creature\u2019s yellow eye opens, its limbs convulse, and Victor recoils from the grotesque reality of what he has made. Fleeing in terror, he suffers nightmares blending love, death, and decay, then encounters the creature looming over his bed. He escapes into the night and wanders the streets in dread. The next morning brings temporary relief when his friend Henry Clerval arrives, but Victor\u2019s mind collapses under the strain. He falls into a months\u2011long nervous fever, haunted by visions of the monster. This chapter marks the catastrophic birth of the Creature and Victor\u2019s immediate moral and psychological failure\u2014he abandons his creation the moment it lives.\nVictor animates the creature, recoils in horror, falls ill from shock, and is nursed by Henry Clerval until spring.\nThemes: creation and responsibility, horror of the unnatural, scientific ambition, guilt and revulsion, friendship and care", "vector": [-0.0018346684519201517, -0.02208765037357807, 0.0027479329146444798, -0.014468973502516747, 0.001849319669418037, 0.013081983663141727, -0.01924855262041092, -0.004385622683912516, -0.003630266757681966, -0.011506155133247375, -0.011929414235055447, 0.03669987991452217, -0.002887934213504195, -0.0012681514490395784, 0.008934037759900093, -0.002536302898079157, 0.04438367486000061, 0.009266133420169353, 0.016708994284272194, -0.03646545857191086, -0.013388033024966717, 0.017737841233611107, -0.007703328039497137, -0.005567494314163923, -0.01960018463432789, -0.01125871017575264, 0.01832389272749424, -0.03518916666507721, 0.03185518458485603, -0.017373187467455864, -0.007397278677672148, -0.007390767335891724, -0.012574071995913982, -0.004252132959663868, -0.029667256399989128, 0.0009922186145558953, -0.00